1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,505
Payday hits, and for about 48 hours,

2
00:00:03,505 --> 00:00:04,519
you feel rich.

3
00:00:04,859 --> 00:00:06,464
Then rent, subscriptions,

4
00:00:06,464 --> 00:00:09,353
and one little impulse order take everything.

5
00:00:09,693 --> 00:00:12,052
And by the 20th, you're broke again.

6
00:00:12,392 --> 00:00:13,867
If that sounds familiar,

7
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:15,220
this video is for you.

8
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:18,967
Welcome to Broke to Balance,

9
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:21,150
where we turn paychecks into plans.

10
00:00:21,490 --> 00:00:21,879
Today,

11
00:00:21,879 --> 00:00:23,885
you'll learn how to budget your

12
00:00:23,885 --> 00:00:25,891
paycheck using one simple rule,

13
00:00:25,891 --> 00:00:27,314
in under five minutes.

14
00:00:29,314 --> 00:00:29,896
Step one:

15
00:00:29,896 --> 00:00:32,674
budget your take-home pay, not your salary.

16
00:00:33,014 --> 00:00:35,326
Take-home is the money that actually

17
00:00:35,326 --> 00:00:37,061
lands in your bank account,

18
00:00:37,061 --> 00:00:38,795
after taxes and deductions.

19
00:00:39,135 --> 00:00:42,963
In this example, you earn $3,600 a month.

20
00:00:43,303 --> 00:00:46,484
After $600 in taxes and deductions,

21
00:00:46,824 --> 00:00:49,437
$3,000 is yours to plan.

22
00:00:51,437 --> 00:00:53,474
Meet the 50/30/20 rule,

23
00:00:53,474 --> 00:00:57,193
popularized by Elizabeth Warren and Amelia

24
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:00,735
Warren Tyagi in the book All Your Worth.

25
00:01:01,075 --> 00:01:03,998
50% of your take-home goes to needs.

26
00:01:04,338 --> 00:01:06,027
30% goes to wants.

27
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:09,430
And 20% goes to savings and debt payoff.

28
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:14,588
On $3,000, that's $1,500, $900, and $600.

29
00:01:16,588 --> 00:01:19,007
Needs are the bills you can't skip:

30
00:01:19,007 --> 00:01:21,977
rent, groceries, utilities, transportation,

31
00:01:21,977 --> 00:01:22,945
and insurance.

32
00:01:23,285 --> 00:01:25,318
Add them up in a spreadsheet, like this.

33
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:29,551
In our example, needs total $1,500,

34
00:01:29,551 --> 00:01:30,886
exactly 50%.

35
00:01:31,226 --> 00:01:33,525
If yours come in higher, don't panic.

36
00:01:33,525 --> 00:01:35,824
That's not failure, it's information,

37
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:37,750
and it shows you where to trim.

38
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:41,486
Wants are what make life fun:

39
00:01:41,486 --> 00:01:44,060
eating out, streaming, shopping, and trips.

40
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,725
$900 is a real amount of fun,

41
00:01:46,725 --> 00:01:49,612
but only if you decide where it goes

42
00:01:49,612 --> 00:01:51,536
before the month starts.

43
00:01:51,876 --> 00:01:52,915
Here's the trick:

44
00:01:52,915 --> 00:01:55,053
give every want a name and a limit.

45
00:01:57,053 --> 00:02:00,024
The last 20% is what builds your balance.

46
00:02:00,364 --> 00:02:02,664
First, a starter emergency fund.

47
00:02:03,004 --> 00:02:05,034
Then, pay down high-interest debt.

48
00:02:05,374 --> 00:02:07,196
Then, invest for your future.

49
00:02:07,536 --> 00:02:13,046
Saving $600 a month adds up to $7,200 in one year.

50
00:02:13,386 --> 00:02:17,398
Invested for 5 years at an assumed 7%

51
00:02:17,398 --> 00:02:20,977
average return (not a guarantee),

52
00:02:20,977 --> 00:02:24,339
it could grow to about $43,000.

53
00:02:24,679 --> 00:02:26,681
That's the power of consistency.

54
00:02:29,281 --> 00:02:30,957
Three tips to make this stick.

55
00:02:31,297 --> 00:02:31,560
One:

56
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:32,810
pay yourself first.

57
00:02:32,810 --> 00:02:34,783
Automate a transfer on payday,

58
00:02:34,783 --> 00:02:37,940
so your savings leave before you can spend them.

59
00:02:38,280 --> 00:02:38,532
Two:

60
00:02:38,532 --> 00:02:40,612
track it in a simple spreadsheet.

61
00:02:40,612 --> 00:02:42,756
10 minutes a week is all it takes.

62
00:02:43,096 --> 00:02:43,558
Three:

63
00:02:43,558 --> 00:02:46,102
adjust the rule to fit your life.

64
00:02:46,102 --> 00:02:48,492
If rent is high where you live,

65
00:02:48,492 --> 00:02:49,494
try 60/20/20.

66
00:02:49,494 --> 00:02:51,498
Progress beats perfection.

67
00:02:53,498 --> 00:02:54,636
Before we wrap up,

68
00:02:54,636 --> 00:02:57,291
three mistakes that quietly break budgets.

69
00:02:57,631 --> 00:02:58,412
Mistake one:

70
00:02:58,412 --> 00:03:00,690
budgeting your gross salary instead

71
00:03:00,690 --> 00:03:02,122
of your take-home pay.

72
00:03:02,122 --> 00:03:03,945
That makes every number look

73
00:03:03,945 --> 00:03:05,572
bigger than it really is.

74
00:03:05,912 --> 00:03:06,728
Mistake two:

75
00:03:06,728 --> 00:03:08,562
forgetting irregular costs,

76
00:03:08,562 --> 00:03:11,348
like car repairs, gifts, and annual fees.

77
00:03:11,348 --> 00:03:13,387
Set aside a little each month,

78
00:03:13,387 --> 00:03:15,221
so they don't surprise you.

79
00:03:15,561 --> 00:03:16,457
Mistake three:

80
00:03:16,457 --> 00:03:19,206
making your budget so strict that you quit.

81
00:03:19,206 --> 00:03:20,421
Leave room for fun,

82
00:03:20,421 --> 00:03:22,659
because a budget you can stick with

83
00:03:22,659 --> 00:03:24,577
beats a perfect one you can't.

84
00:03:26,577 --> 00:03:27,938
So, here's the recap.

85
00:03:28,278 --> 00:03:29,920
50% for needs.

86
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:31,730
30% for wants.

87
00:03:32,070 --> 00:03:33,903
20% for future you.

88
00:03:34,243 --> 00:03:35,141
If this helped,

89
00:03:35,141 --> 00:03:36,937
subscribe to Broke to Balance,

90
00:03:36,937 --> 00:03:38,613
and tell me in the comments:

91
00:03:38,613 --> 00:03:40,528
which bucket is hardest for you?

92
00:03:40,868 --> 00:03:43,280
This video is for education only,

93
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,254
and isn't financial advice.

94
00:03:45,254 --> 00:03:46,569
See you next time.

